  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA warm front is bringing some scattered light rain showers to \nthe area this morning, most concentrated around the Finger Lakes\nRegion. These showers will lift northward and mostly be north of\nthe region by this afternoon. That being said, a few isolated\nafternoon showers cannot be ruled out along and north the NY\nThruway Corridor.\n\nOtherwise, today will feature temperatures several degrees \nabove normal for early October standards, with highs mainly in \nthe 70s. Right now the expectation is that most of the day will \nbe mainly cloudy, but partial clearing is possible later this \nafternoon. If this clearing happens earlier, then temperatures \ncould be higher than currently forecasted and some of the warmer\nvalleys could reach 80 degrees.\n\nA cold front will drop in from the northwest on Friday, which\nwill bring rain showers to the area once again. Instability will\nbe lacking, so thunderstorms are not expected at this time from\nthe front. Temperatures on Friday will vary, depending on\nlocation. For most of Central NY north of Interstate 88, the\nearlier cold front passage will likely keep highs in the 60s.\nHowever, farther south (Northeast PA to the Catskills), the \nlater cold front passage will likely result in highs mainly in\nthe 70s, and perhaps near 80 degrees in the Wyoming Valley.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nHigh pressure will bring fantastic fall-weather to the area on\nSaturday with seasonably cool temperatures (highs mainly in the\n60s) and plenty of sunshine.\n\nOn Sunday, our region will be sandwiched between a low pressure\nsystem passing by well to the south and a trough dipping down\nfrom Canada. The most likely outcome is mainly dry conditions\nfor the area, but a few rain showers may sneak into Northeast\nPA-Catskills from the southern system. Otherwise, Sunday will be\npartly to mostly sunny with highs in the 60s once again.\n\nMuch cooler weather looks to move into the region early next\nweek as the influence from a deep longwave trough dipping\nsouthward from Canada becomes more apparent. With a surface high\npressure system likely in place, the first widespread \nfrost/freeze is possible if skies can remain clear Monday night \nand especially Tuesday night.",
